  "title": "The indoor sound environment and human task performance: A literature review on the role of room acoustics",
  "authors": "Reinten, J. et al., 2017",
  "review_question": "To what extent can current evidence on the effect of sound in the work environment on human performance be used to aid room acoustic design decisions?",
  "inclusion_exclusion_criteria": "Included studies contained empirical evidence on the influence of sound/noise on human performance, subjects were 18-65 years old and healthy, and the difference between control and experimental conditions was attributable to a passive room acoustic change. Specific criteria for sound level differences (max 25 dB for single source, 11 dB for multitalker/broadband, 6 dB for other) and exclusion of completely silent conditions or active masking systems were applied. Excluded were indirect effects, review papers, non-English papers, studies with sound levels >85 dB(A), or speech intelligibility created in a non-room acoustic manner.",
